{"reference":"Isaiah 54","book":"Isaiah","chapter":54,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions of Interpretation","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Paul later describes his use of Hagar and Sarah as an allegory, mapping women, sons, mountains, covenants, and Jerusalems within a polemical argument. His construction is relevant to discussions of this opening verse, but its details remain contested.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 54:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_054_86b18cd4930f4dc454aa3ada"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter closes by calling this promise the heritage of the servants of Jehovah, using a plural term. Elsewhere in Isaiah, servant language can refer to Jacob or Israel and to a plural community, while singular servant poems have prompted differing identifications. This makes it important not to assume that every use of “servant” in Isaiah has the same referent.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 54:17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_054_afba528b6cc46f9a241a8756"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Book of Isaiah in Transmission","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The Great Isaiah Scroll is an almost complete witness to all sixty-six chapters of Isaiah. It broadly aligns with the Masoretic tradition while also containing thousands of spelling and substantive differences; other Qumran Isaiah manuscripts and interpretive writings show that the book was actively copied and interpreted.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_054_e5e018f5b4ba1636b11f0546"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}